kosha foundation

  -- the part of a structure that transfers all loads to the ground.
  -- the ground is the ultimate support — every load ends here.
  -- foundation design depends on: total load, ground bearing capacity, soil type.
  --
  -- bearing capacity: the maximum pressure the ground can support without failure.
  --   rock: 3000-10000 kPa. dense gravel: 200-600 kPa. soft clay: 25-75 kPa.
  --   if stress > bearing capacity: settlement or collapse.
  --
  -- types:
  --   shallow foundation (footing): spreads load over larger area (σ = F/A reduced).
  --                                  used when bearing capacity is adequate near surface.
  --   deep foundation (pile):       drives/bores into deeper, stronger ground.
  --                                  used for soft soil, heavy loads, tall buildings.
  --   raft foundation:              one large slab under entire building.
  --                                  distributes load evenly. reduces differential settlement.
  --
  -- settlement: ground compresses slightly under load → building sinks slowly.
  -- differential settlement: uneven sinking → cracking. Tower of Pisa: differential.
  --
  -- the foundation is the interface between built structure and the earth.
  -- it is where civil engineering meets geology.

